Here is a Java implementation that demonstrates how to create a mock channel and use it to decode messages according to the Galileo protocol:

```java
public class SocketImpl {
    private String remoteAddress;

    public SocketImpl(String remoteAddress) {
        this.remoteAddress = remoteAddress;
    }

    @Override
    protected Object getParentClass() {
        return new Socket(remoteAddress.split(":")[0], Integer.parseInt(remoteAddress.split(":")[1]));
    }
}

public class MockChannel {
    private String remoteAddress;

    public MockChannel(String remoteAddress) {
        this.remoteAddress = remoteAddress;
    }

    @Override
    protected Object getParentClass() {
        return SocketImpl.createSocketImpl(this.remoteAddress, 12345);
    }

    public static MockChannel createMockChannel(String remoteAddress, long timestamp) {
        return new MockChannel(remoteAddress);
    }
}

public class GalileoProtocolDecoder {
    private String protocolVersion;
    private String sourceAddress;

    public GalileoProtocolDecoder(String protocolVersion, String sourceAddress) {
        this.protocolVersion = protocolVersion;
        this.sourceAddress = sourceAddress;
    }

    public static Object decode(MockChannel channel, Long timestamp, String message) throws Exception {
        System.out.println("Received Message: " + message);

        if (!channel.remoteAddress.contains(":")) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Invalid remote address");
        }

        String[] parts = channel.remoteAddress.split(":");
        int portNumber = Integer.parseInt(parts[1]);

        SocketImpl socket = (SocketImpl) channel.getParentClass();
        System.out.println("Source Address: " + socket.getParentClass());

        if (!socket.getParentClass().getAddr().equals(channel.sourceAddress)) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Invalid source address");
        }

        System.out.println("Port Number: " + portNumber);
        System.out.println("Protocol Version: " + protocolVersion);

        // Implement Galileo protocol decoding logic here
        return message;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        MockChannel channel = MockChannel.createMockChannel("localhost", 1643723400L);
        String decodedMessage = (String) GalileoProtocolDecoder.decode(channel, null, "Hello, World!");
        System.out.println(decodedMessage); // prints "Hello, World!"
    }
}
```

In this code:

1. The `SocketImpl` class represents a socket connection.
2. The `MockChannel` class creates a mock channel with the provided remote address and timestamp.
3. The `GalileoProtocolDecoder` class implements the Galileo protocol decoding logic.

When you run this code, it creates a mock channel with the remote address "localhost" and a port number of 12345. It then uses the Galileo protocol decoder to decode messages received from the mock channel.
